Subject: [RFC PATCH net-next 0/6] ionic: support netmem TX

This series lets a dma-buf be bound to an ionic netdev for transmit
(NETDEV_CMD_BIND_TX) and sent from with sendmsg(MSG_ZEROCOPY) and
SCM_DEVMEM_DMABUF, as bnxt, gve, mlx5 and fbnic allow.

It is an RFC because it has only been built so far, and it is still
based on v7.4-rc3 and not on net-next (see the end for both).

Patch 1 is a fix for an existing bug in the XDP_REDIRECT error path that
could go to net on its own; it is here because the stale state it leaves
is in the descriptor info the next patches rely on. Patches 2 and 3 make
the Tx unmap paths safe for net_iov frags. ionic already maps with
skb_frag_dma_map(); only the unmap side needed work. Patch 4 counts the
unreadable skbs the driver drops. Patch 6 sets netdev->netmem_tx if the
device has Tx scatter-gather and checksum offload.

Patch 5 is not for net-next. On a tree without commit 23fe6ac2e97a
("net: skbuff: make skb_copy_and_csum_bits() report unreadable frags"),
such as v7.4-rc3, software GSO of an unreadable skb with NETIF_F_SG and
NETIF_F_HW_CSUM both off sends zeroes with a valid checksum, and patch 5
makes ionic drop those packets. Nothing else in the series depends on
it: leave it out when rebasing onto net-next.

Is this a driver-only change?

Yes. Nothing in the core needs to change for ionic: the bind needs only
netmem_tx and a parent device with a DMA mask, and every helper the
driver uses is already there (skb_frag_dma_map(),
netmem_dma_unmap_addr_set(), netmem_dma_unmap_page_attrs(),
skb_frags_readable()). netmem_tx is set once before the netdev is
registered, as in the other drivers.

One limitation is left that a driver can't lift. Nothing bounds the
frags of a devmem skb by what the device can take. ionic sends at most
16 buffers per packet (head plus 15 SG elements), an skb has up to
MAX_SKB_FRAGS frags, and a devmem sendmsg() makes one frag per iovec
entry per page. A sender using pieces smaller than about MSS/16 builds
packets the driver can neither send nor linearize, so they are dropped
and the connection stalls. ndo_features_check() can't help (it can't
split a non-GSO skb, and a software GSO segment keeps the same frags).
This is what gve does today as well: the same fallback to
skb_linearize() and the same drop. NETDEV_CMD_BIND_TX is unprivileged,
but the sender can only hurt its own connection. An error from
sendmsg() would still be better than a stall; that takes a per-netdev
limit applied where the core builds the skb, which I propose as
follow-up work, not as part of this series. Patch 4 makes the drop
visible.

The ethtool counter in patch 4 is driver-private, and no other netmem
Tx driver has one. If such a count should rather be a netdev queue
stat, or not exist, patch 4 can be reworked or dropped: only patch 5
mentions it.

Things I noticed in ionic that this series leaves alone, since none of
them is specific to netmem:

 - max_sg_elems is set to 15 for every Tx queue, also when the device
   reports the 8 element SG descriptor.
 - After a firmware reset the features the device grants are re-read but
   netdev->features and, with this series, netmem_tx are not recomputed.
 - Addresses are masked to 52 bits when they go into the descriptor, with
   no check. A TSO segment that starts inside a frag puts a frag address
   there.

Base: v7.4-rc3. The patches other than 5 also apply, unchanged, to the
ionic files of net-next main as of 2026-11-18 (d6816d9a93b6), but they
were not built there. v7.4-rc3 lacks two things net-next has: the
skb_segment() fix named above, and net_devmem_dev_unregister(), without
which a Tx binding keeps a pointer to a netdev that was unregistered
under it.

Testing: build only. x86_64, gcc 13, W=1 and sparse, at every patch, and
once more with the dma_unmap_addr macros compiled away. Nothing has been
run: no devmem.py, no ncdevmem, no feature toggling.

Stephen Bates (6):
  ionic: don't leave a failed XDP frame in the Tx descriptor info
  ionic: give Tx buffers their own bookkeeping struct
  ionic: unmap Tx frags with the netmem helpers
  ionic: count the unreadable skbs dropped on Tx
  ionic: don't send an unreadable skb that has no frags
  ionic: add netmem TX support

